#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231839} :activity/id #uuid "b8c77116-13b1-5351-9553-d047fa93cd04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049590 :topic/id #uuid "02cc8363-d667-570b-9679-eb87a01109a9" :topic/math-academy-id 6728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231995 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we solved second-order initial value problems using Laplace transforms when the forcing term was an ordinary function of $t.$\n\nIn this lesson, we extend the same method to **time-delayed forcing**, where the right-hand side turns on at a specific time. This type of forcing is modeled using the **unit step function**.\n\nFor a fixed constant $a>0,$ we write $$u_a(t) = u(t-a),$$ which represents a signal that is zero for $t< a$ and turns on at $t = a.$ For example, $u(t-3)$ remains zero until $t=3,$ at which point it jumps to $1.$\n\nWhen a second-order differential equation includes a term involving $u_a(t),$ we still begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and solving for $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ (for $s>0$). The left-hand side is handled exactly as before, using the Laplace transforms of derivatives and the given initial conditions.\n\nThe main new idea is how the unit step function affects the transform of the forcing term. We will see how to account for this delay and how it changes the algebraic equation for $Y(s).$ To do this, we first need to determine the transform of the step function itself. Let's find some Laplace transforms." :tutorial/id #uuid "932cf723-31f5-5d76-95e4-d384feb59c11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14810 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3d26acf-ed42-5d5f-9088-64b3a8a9e309" :step/math-academy-id 612666 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232000 :tutorial/content "Consider a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing, such as $$y'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n**Step 1: Transform the left-hand side.**\n\nWe begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides. Let $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$ Using the formula for the second derivative and the initial conditions, we have $$\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}=s^2Y(s)-sy(0)-y'(0) = s^2Y(s).$$\n\n**Step 2: Transform the right-hand side.**\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, the unit step function satisfies $$\\mathcal L\\{u_2(t)\\}=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s},$$ which is valid for $s>0.$\n\n**Step 3: Solve for $Y(s).$**\n\nSubstituting these results into the transformed equation gives $$s^2Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}.$$ We now collect terms and solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s({s}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAside from the exponential factor $e^{-2s},$ the steps are exactly the same as in problems without time delay." :tutorial/id #uuid "f7be86fb-5367-564f-906a-5ce064205685" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14834 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "f07d73a0-6354-56ab-81e4-4b5131af3c8c" :step/math-academy-id 612667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231432 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231403 :question/id #uuid "7ff7cc10-3649-5ffe-9a8a-6aedd7954ea5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6664"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0baa73e5-19ee-5a80-bd02-cc9678615d60" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049950}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377452}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of a Second-Order ODE With Step Function Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "8145dfa8-c039-550b-be43-1fa940e25360" :step/math-academy-id 612668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231998 :tutorial/content "In the first-order case, we saw that a time delay in the forcing term appears in the $s$-domain as a factor of $e^{-as},$ and that we can handle this using the second shifting theorem.\n\nThe same idea applies when working with **second-order** differential equations.\n\nConsider the initial value problem $$y'' + 4y = 7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}, \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nAgain, we begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and writing $$Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$$ Using the initial conditions, the left-hand side becomes $$\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}+4\\mathcal L\\{y\\}=s^2Y(s)+4Y(s).$$\n\nThe forcing term has the form $$u_2(t)\\,f(t-2),$$ where $$f(t-2)=7e^{-2(t-2)} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad f(t)=7e^{-2t}.$$\n\nAs in the first-order case, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. That is, if $$F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\},$$ then $$\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}F(s),$$ which is valid for $s>0.$\n\nHere, using $a=2,$ we have $$F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{7e^{-2t}\\}=\\frac{7}{s+2},$$ so the transformed forcing term is $$\\mathcal L\\{7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}\\} = e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{7}{s+2}.$$\n\nSubstituting into the transformed differential equation gives $$s^2Y(s)+4Y(s)=\\frac{7e^{-2s}}{s+2}.$$ We now solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-2s}}{s+2} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-2s}}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+4)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nJust as in the first-order case, the delay introduces the factor $e^{-as}.$ Once this factor is identified, the remaining steps follow the same algebraic process used for second-order equations without time delay." :tutorial/id #uuid "b7d1588f-46ce-5a23-a449-ceb6b8f25099" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14829 :tutorial/title "Time-Delayed Forcing and the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "b88d3621-4e32-596f-9b38-b53f3da4d2ad" :step/math-academy-id 612671 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231301 :question/id #uuid "9941d046-be37-5ada-8d98-ecedfcb9d7f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21904"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15ae93ce-c4dd-5b33-b9ff-381502364213" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049492}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518201}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of a Second-Order ODE With Time-Delayed Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "0947db02-2bd3-50a0-803f-4e78e3ce7311" :step/math-academy-id 612672 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231999 :tutorial/content "Once the Laplace transform has been found, solving a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing follows the same overall strategy as before: we take the inverse Laplace transform.\n\nThe key difference is handling terms that include a factor of $e^{-as}$.\n\nConsider the initial value problem $$y'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nWe previously found the Laplace transform $$Y(s)=e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}.$$\n\nTo take the inverse Laplace transform, we first focus on the *base function* $$F(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}.$$ Using partial fraction decomposition, we write $$\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}.$$\n\nNow we compute the inverse transform of $F(s)$ and call it $f(t){:}$ $$f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\!\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}\\right\\} = 1-\\cos t.$$\n\nNext, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. Since $Y(s)=e^{-2s}F(s)$, we have $$\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{e^{-2s}F(s)\\} = u(t-2)\\cdot f(t-2).$$ Substituting our result for $f(t)$, the solution to the initial value problem is $$y(t)=u(t-2)\\left(1-\\cos(t-2)\\right).$$\n\nThe important idea is that once the exponential factor $e^{-as}$ has been identified, we first find the inverse transform of the base function, then shift the result and multiply by the unit step function." :tutorial/id #uuid "10f6f136-0604-57e5-b635-1c8a5cfcf8da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14830 :tutorial/title "Solving for the Laplace Transform With Step Function Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "3dff1d7e-fcee-5906-8723-2dbeac9f85ee" :step/math-academy-id 612676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231434 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231300 :question/id #uuid "029321f3-c767-5a77-95d7-5b4150e24f42" :question/math-academy-id "e-21903"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0459cf03-27db-5198-ab3a-1dbeecb675a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518206}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Initial Value Problem With Step Function Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "f8a1577f-f7d4-5c2b-869b-f0ae315317fa" :step/math-academy-id 612677}] :activity/title "Solving Second-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231525}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518311} :activity/id #uuid "8b4c0c6e-e1ad-53be-980f-1125809beccc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049590 :topic/id #uuid "02cc8363-d667-570b-9679-eb87a01109a9" :topic/math-academy-id 6728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070cb125-df6a-5a6e-a3c1-eebd12e71978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377475} {:answer/id #uuid "f4013812-37ba-5064-ab55-54d4b4b78e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-2s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377476} {:answer/id #uuid "b3afdc87-a5c1-502d-b40f-2421236c8cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} {:answer/id #uuid "436278c2-b580-52a1-8e49-b07bf5e41e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+4s+13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377478} {:answer/id #uuid "665b8f05-7e39-58e8-bd72-72806cf6ff9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{9{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} :answer-field/id #uuid "443034ac-6f38-5490-9b31-4ff0cd1c4442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c8533a8-1f90-5b54-be02-41e34f166ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345255" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1)),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y\\} & =L\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+4L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+13L\\{y\\} & =L\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the first derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand side, we use the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nHere, $a=1$ and $f(t-1)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))$, so $f(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$ The transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\}=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nApplying the shift, we get\n\n$$\nL\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\}={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+4sY(s)+13Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+4s+13)Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ed0242a-2db9-5144-8e11-5ba6887b8361" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518313}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9575d503-b33a-5aaf-a793-2497c79dc90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{-5(t-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517769} :answer-field/id #uuid "5479ca0e-e235-5daf-9f78-3a7ac798d247" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e0f1706f-269a-5658-8fd3-75993082b4c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345300" :question/problem "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+6{y}^{′}(t)+5y(t)=20{u}_{4}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s)=\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ (s+1)(s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{s+5}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=4,$ $B=-5,$ and $C=1,$ giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5},\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nf(t)=4-5{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-4s}F(s)\\} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{4}(t)\\,f(t-4) \\\\ y(t) & =u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}+{e}^{-5(t-4)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99b6edeb-bbfc-5c8b-b1c4-dd5efc3764aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a207cf-6ec4-5e6d-997d-39d88a243b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517496} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4db7a8-b2f3-5998-9cda-8fbb2aabb05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517567} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf6de9-76a1-580c-a0a5-04e50833b026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3s+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517752} {:answer/id #uuid "8f14cb64-f15c-5e63-97dc-cb8d52fe503e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517757} {:answer/id #uuid "a5df1187-9902-5a00-900d-f4f5ec5cd0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{s({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517496} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d0813c-5c54-5890-9610-b5222646eaaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2f59173-bdb2-59d3-8bec-268e720e4bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345246" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{4}(t)=u(t-4)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+16y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=4$ and $f(t)=7(t-4).$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{7u(t-4)\\cdot (t-4)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7((t+4)-4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7t\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot 7\\cdot \\frac{1!}{{s}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)(t-4)\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-3+16Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+16)Y(s) & =3+\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b8b7f1db-a884-5b7a-8f66-cefcb30e2b44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462f1b1b-9444-58ff-b801-8fbbfcbcb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377461} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe9218e-9c07-5797-9e3d-2114ea136be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377462} {:answer/id #uuid "1c30168a-f94d-52a2-9a98-bf5ae26c54ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377463} {:answer/id #uuid "5d96461b-b5bf-5858-b6b1-4e9dc9828ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} {:answer/id #uuid "b67556e4-5189-5cc7-aaee-81b6db402e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4669c7-fc85-5f41-a5f5-f8f6933fbb16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e512d666-26fd-5562-9dad-5a955b3c727f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345242" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+9y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+9L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=5(t-3)^{2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{5u(t-3)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5((t+3)-3)^{2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5{t}^{2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+9L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)(t-3)^{2}\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)+9Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+9)Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e9df5258-7677-5050-9ab9-8650f43b3255" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c35b6c1e-f747-5862-8863-fe053c5aa6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377482} {:answer/id #uuid "143176f2-5f66-5c47-bc95-252c5655a68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\cos(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517527} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c74efd7-0f5a-5f39-afee-3b02924def1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3731bc55-d35c-5b26-9516-425a707e0d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-345263" :question/problem "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+y(t)={u}_{1}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-1)\\,\\cdot \\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=1$, $B=-1$, and $C=0$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nf(t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1})\\} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{1}(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-1)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb271466-d784-5171-b57b-f15347d64110" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518277}}] :activity/title "Solving Second-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517488}]}
